,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,1,1,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,4,4,7,7,7,1,1,7,7,7,7],[7,7,6,6,7,4,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,6,6,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,7,7,7,1,1],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,7,7,7,1,1],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,3,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,6,7,7,7,3,3,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,6,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,3,3,7,7,7,7,7],[7,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,3,3,7,7,7,7,7]],"output":[[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,4,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,4,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,7,7,1,1,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,4,4,7,7,7,1,1,7,7,7,7],[7,7,6,6,7,4,4,7,7,7,7,7,1,7,7,7],[7,7,6,6,7,7,7,7,8,8,8,7,7,1,7,7],[7,7,7,7,6,7,7,7,8,8,8,7,7,7,1,1],[7,7,7,7,7,6,7,7,8,8,8,7,7,7,1,1],[7,7,7,7,7,7,6,8,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,8,6,7,7,7,7,7,3,3,7],[7,7,7,7,7,8,7,7,6,6,7,7,7,3,3,7],[7,7,7,7,8,7,7,7,6,6,7,7,3,7,7,7],[7,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3,7,7,7,7],[7,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,3,3,7,7,7,7,7],[7,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,3,3,7,7,7,7,7]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grids are made of.** Each grid has a background of 7 and a few solid squares, either 2×2 or 3×3. Every colour appears exactly twice. In each pair, the two same-coloured squares sit on a common diagonal. For example, in train 2 the 8-squares occupy rows/cols 2–4 and 11–13, and the 9-squares are at (9–10, 1–2) and (14–15, 6–7).\n\n**What changes.** The fact sheet lists the added cells as straight diagonal segments, one per colour. Each segment starts next to one square of that colour and ends next to its twin. So the rule is: **for each colour, draw a one-cell-wide diagonal line in that colour connecting the facing corners of its two squares.**\n\nChecks against the demonstrations:\n- Train 1, colour 4: the squares are at (6–7, 14–15) and (14–15, 6–7). The line starts just outside the bottom-left corner of the upper square, at (8,13). It runs down-left to (13,8), which is right next to the lower square's corner at (14,7). That is 6 cells, matching the fact sheet.\n- Train 2, colour 8: the line goes down-right from (5,5) to (10,10). It fills the gap between the boxes [2,2,4,4] and [11,11,13,13].\n- Crossing lines are interleaved, e.g. the 8 and 4 lines in train 2, or the 3 and 5 lines in train 3. They pass through each other without ever sharing a cell. Painting only on background cells is therefore enough.\n\n**Construction.**\n1. Group the objects by colour, so each colour gives a pair.\n2. Take the object that is higher up as A and the other as B. The step direction is dy = +1, and dx is the sign of B's left x minus A's left x.\n3. Start at the corner of A's bounding box, one cell outside it diagonally in direction (dy, dx).\n4. Paint background cells until the walk enters B's bounding box.\n5. Emit one stage per drawn line, then emit and return the final canvas.\n\n**Applying it to the test input.** Five colour pairs give five lines:\n- **4:** from (2,8) down-left to (3,7), reaching the square at (4–5, 5–6).\n- **1:** from (5,12) down-right to (6,13), reaching (7–8, 14–15).\n- **6:** from (7,4) down-right to (10,7), reaching (11–12, 8–9).\n- **3:** from (12,12) down-left to (13,11), reaching (14–15, 9–10).\n- **8:** from (9,7) down-left to (12,4), reaching (13–15, 1–3).","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid).copy()\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    bycol = {}\n    for o in objs:\n        bycol.setdefault(o[\"colour\"], []).append(o)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    for c in sorted(bycol):\n        lst = bycol[c]\n        if len(lst) != 2:\n            continue\n        a, b = sorted(lst, key=lambda o: (o[\"box\"][0], o[\"box\"][1]))\n        ay0, ax0, ay1, ax1 = a[\"box\"]\n        by0, bx0, by1, bx1 = b[\"box\"]\n        dy = 1\n        dx = 1 if bx0 > ax0 else -1\n        y = ay1 + 1\n        x = ax1 + 1 if dx == 1 else ax0 - 1\n        while 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W and not (by0 <= y <= by1 and bx0 <= x <= bx1):\n            if g[y, x] == bg:\n                g[y, x] = c\n            y += dy\n            x += dx\n        emit(g, f\"Connect the two colour-{c} squares with a diagonal line of colour {c}.\")\n    emit(g, \"All same-coloured pairs are connected.\")\n    return g\n","candidate_tactics":{"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Start at the corner of A's bounding box, one cell outside it diagonally in direction (dy, dx)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Paint background cells until the walk enters B's bounding box."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"0bd3acc0c84802db2b40490c51019483efd8d4b3266c8f17a11ca6f27564fec0","task_sha256":"f51af380bdece26ff4305a773b663d74094c17d3a423cfaf5e869702418a09df","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
